Open the file for w with open() rather than creat().
writeLines created its output file with creat(). On Windows that is the
C runtime's own function, which knows nothing of the device names that
mingw_open handles, so "w /dev/null" tried to create a file named null
in a directory \dev and usually failed, although the file operand and r
accepted the same names. Use the equivalent open() call, which goes
through mingw_open there and is unchanged elsewhere.

---
 editors/ed.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/ed.c
+++ b/editors/ed.c
@@ -508,7 +508,7 @@
 
 	charCount = 0;
 
-	fd = creat(file, 0666);
+	fd = open(file, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0666);
 	if (fd < 0) {
 		bb_simple_perror_msg(file);
 		return FALSE;
